      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This doesn\u0027t prevent someone from changing the property later, does it?\n\nThat\u0027s by design; we should not break apps that try to set the property. They should still be able to set handlers; what we want is to keep the handlers no-op.\n\nWhen the app sets a new handler, that handler never gets invoked. Behind the scenes, the browser still examines the original object, finds no event handler there, ~et voilà~ and nothing happens.\n\nYou\u0027re welcome to test this. Run this code from DevTools and play with your connection; you\u0027ll see that \"online\" and \"offline\" fire, but the \"change\" event does not fire.\n```\n// navigator.connection and associated event.\n(function () {\n  class MockConnection extends EventTarget {\n    constructor() {\n      super();\n      this.effectiveType \u003d \u00274g\u0027;\n      this.rtt \u003d 100;\n      this.downlink \u003d 5;\n      this.saveData \u003d false;\n      this.onchange \u003d null;\n    }\n  }\n\n  Object.defineProperty(navigator, \"connection\", {\n    value: new MockConnection(),\n    configurable: true\n  });\n})();\n\nnavigator.connection.onchange \u003d () \u003d\u003e console.log(\"Some change.\");\nwindow.addEventListener(\"online\", () \u003d\u003e console.log(\"Online.\"));\nwindow.addEventListener(\"offline\", () \u003d\u003e console.log(\"Offline.\"));\n```",
